WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:05.202
On the surface, everything at the March home appeared to return to normal.

2
00:00:05.203 --> 00:00:11.584
Amy was back home, showing off her ring (not mentioning to Meg that she had left it to her in her will)

3
00:00:11.585 --> 00:00:16.975
and entertaining the family with funny episodes about Aunt March and her parrot, Polly.

4
00:00:16.976 --> 00:00:22.354
Beneath the smiles and laughter, however, the sisters had changed considerably;

5
00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:27.243
they were no longer the carefree young girls they used to be before Beth's illness.

6
00:00:27.244 --> 00:00:31.386
Perhaps the person who changed the most was Beth herself.

7
00:00:31.387 --> 00:00:36.814
Outwardly, she seemed to regain her health and sunny disposition.

8
00:00:36.815 --> 00:00:45.271
Her optimistic and innocent world, however, had been shattered when the Hummel baby died in her arms, giving Beth a close encounter with death.

9
00:00:45.272 --> 00:00:51.616
Beth withdrew even more into herself as if an inner light in her had been switched off.

10
00:00:51.617 --> 00:01:00.475
Seeing the change in Beth, Jo knew that she could never forgive herself for contributing to the loss of happiness in her sister's life.

11
00:01:00.476 --> 00:01:04.700
"Jo? Jo!" Amy called sharply.

12
00:01:04.701 --> 00:01:09.486
Lost in thought, Jo did not even hear Amy speaking to her.

13
00:01:09.487 --> 00:01:13.862
"Sorry, Amy. What did you say?" she asked.

14
00:01:13.863 --> 00:01:19.921
"I asked, 'Where is Laurie?' He was incredibly faithful," said Amy,

15
00:01:19.922 --> 00:01:24.948
"visiting me every day at Aunt March's so I wouldn't go insane with worry.

16
00:01:24.949 --> 00:01:36.425
Without him, I would have died!" Then realizing her poor choice of words, she quickly added, "I mean, it would have been just awful!"

17
00:01:36.426 --> 00:01:40.139
"Am I his keeper?" barked Jo spitefully.

18
00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:43.898
"Frankly, Laurie is the least of my concerns now.

19
00:01:43.899 --> 00:01:48.500
My thoughts are occupied with far more important matters at the moment."

20
00:01:48.501 --> 00:01:57.533
Meg, noticing Jo's peculiar response, knew that her sister was not on speaking terms with Laurie because of the tasteless joke he had played.

21
00:01:57.534 --> 00:02:06.492
Meg also knew that Jo, although she was pretending to the world and herself that it didn't matter, was deeply troubled by the rift in their friendship.

22
00:02:06.493 --> 00:02:15.010
"I think we should have Laurie and his grandfather over for dinner tonight," Meg suggested while she looked away from Jo's piercing glare.

23
00:02:15.011 --> 00:02:17.944
"Why should we?" Jo asked sharply.

24
00:02:17.945 --> 00:02:24.958
"Jo! You ought to be ashamed of yourself after all the Laurences have done for us," scolded Amy righteously.

25
00:02:24.959 --> 00:02:29.480
"We are indebted to them forever for their selfless deeds."

26
00:02:29.481 --> 00:02:35.335
"I agree with Meg," said Mother, giving Amy a warning look to go easy on Jo.

27
00:02:35.336 --> 00:02:39.167
"We should invite them this evening if they're available."

28
00:02:39.168 --> 00:02:44.562
"Can I ask Laurie myself?" Amy jumped up, clapping her hands like a little girl.

29
00:02:44.563 --> 00:02:49.244
Glancing at Jo, Mother replied, "Yes, you may."

30
00:02:49.245 --> 00:02:53.181
When Mother and Amy left together with Meg trailing closely behind,

31
00:02:53.188 --> 00:02:59.470
Beth turned to her sister with worried eyes and asked, "Is everything all right with you and Laurie?"

32
00:02:59.471 --> 00:03:05.974
Pressing her lips together, Jo forced a smile and took Beth's pale, thin hand.

33
00:03:05.975 --> 00:03:11.639
"Friendships are forged through time, experience, and overcoming obstacles.

34
00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:19.860
Laurie and I have hit a barrier at the moment," Jo said truthfully, "but I'm confident that we will get over this."

35
00:03:19.861 --> 00:03:26.207
Squeezing her hand gently, Beth rested her head on Jo's shoulder.

36
00:03:26.208 --> 00:03:32.706
On such short notice, a lot of tightly organized teamwork was needed to put together a meal quickly.

37
00:03:32.707 --> 00:03:40.573
For the rest of the day, everyone busily took charge of certain tasks to ensure a highly hospitable evening for their neighbors.

38
00:03:40.574 --> 00:03:44.291
"It's half-past six already! They'll be here any minute!"

39
00:03:44.292 --> 00:03:48.599
Amy panicked as she set the cutlery on the table.

40
00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:53.999
"Meg, did you check the potatoes?" Mother asked as she rushed toward the kitchen.

41
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:01.674
"They're all done," answered Meg, careful not to collide with Mother as she carried out a large steaming dish.

42
00:04:01.675 --> 00:04:10.542
While the others scrambled around the kitchen, Jo volunteered to help Beth downstairs so she could join the festivities.

43
00:04:10.543 --> 00:04:17.481
"My legs feel like rubber," giggled Beth as she struggled to maintain her balance on shaky legs.

44
00:04:17.482 --> 00:04:23.052
"Just hang on to me," said Jo, holding Beth around the waist and arm.

45
00:04:23.053 --> 00:04:33.038
Her progress was painstakingly slow, but in the end Beth managed to seat herself on the sofa, then Jo wrapped a thick blanket around her for warmth.

46
00:04:33.039 --> 00:04:40.626
As Beth smiled up at her sister, there was a knock at the door, and Jo was the only one available to answer it.

47
00:04:40.627 --> 00:04:46.104
"Good evening, Mr. Laurence," Jo greeted Laurie's grandfather as she opened the door.

48
00:04:46.105 --> 00:04:50.195
She merely nodded to Laurie, who returned the curt gesture.

49
00:04:50.196 --> 00:04:58.033
Mr. Laurence immediately made his way toward Beth, who welcomed her dear friend with a weak but genuine smile.

50
00:04:58.034 --> 00:05:02.466
Standing together awkwardly, Laurie and Jo spoke at the same time.

51
00:05:02.467 --> 00:05:06.214
"So how. . .," "What did. . .,"

52
00:05:06.215 --> 00:05:08.811
"You go first," said Jo.

53
00:05:08.812 --> 00:05:11.718
"It's nothing," replied Laurie.

54
00:05:11.719 --> 00:05:17.728
"Laurie!" Amy shouted cheerfully as she ran over to him and threw her arms around his neck.

55
00:05:17.729 --> 00:05:22.521
Grateful for the interruption, Jo escaped to help Meg set the table.

56
00:05:22.522 --> 00:05:30.303
Dinner talk was light and full of laughter—the main entertainment was Amy, who repeated her stories from Aunt March's.

57
00:05:30.304 --> 00:05:35.415
If anyone noticed the tension between Laurie and Jo, they did not mention it.

58
00:05:35.416 --> 00:05:40.049
"Jo, can you bring some more bread from the oven?" Mother asked kindly.

59
00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:45.872
Jo stood up, grabbed the bread basket and was heading toward the kitchen when she overheard Mr. Laurence saying,

60
00:05:45.873 --> 00:05:50.234
"Laurie, can you bring me a glass of water?"

61
00:05:50.235 --> 00:05:56.746
Even though Mother protested that one of the girls could get it, Mr. Laurence insisted his grandson should go,

62
00:05:56.747 --> 00:06:00.187
thereby driving Laurie into the kitchen where Jo was.

63
00:06:00.188 --> 00:06:07.000
Trying to ignore him, Jo tossed the bread into the basket as quickly as she could without acknowledging his presence.

64
00:06:07.001 --> 00:06:12.989
But since they were thrown together in a situation like this, they couldn't avoid talking.

65
00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:15.697
"Jo?" Laurie spoke first.

66
00:06:15.698 --> 00:06:17.860
She turned to him without speaking.

67
00:06:17.861 --> 00:06:22.129
"I don't know how to make things right," he said quietly.

68
00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:25.530
"Could you forgive me and accept me as a friend again?"

69
00:06:25.531 --> 00:06:31.681
Jo felt a lump in her throat as she looked at Laurie bowing his head, staring down at his hands.

70
00:06:31.682 --> 00:06:36.146
All her animosity, anger, and hurt melted away in an instant.

71
00:06:36.147 --> 00:06:40.875
"Silly boy," she mumbled, "of course I can."

72
00:06:40.876 --> 00:06:45.292
Jo grabbed him roughly by the neck and playfully messed up his hair.

73
00:06:45.293 --> 00:06:49.167
"I'm really sorry, Jo," Laurie said sincerely.

74
00:06:49.168 --> 00:06:52.952
"Let's just forget about it," said Jo.

75
00:06:52.953 --> 00:06:57.872
The two friends smiled at each other and knew that everything was going to be fine.

76
00:06:57.873 --> 00:07:04.102
Suddenly a loud commotion arose from the living room, forcing Jo and Laurie to cut short their reunion.

77
00:07:04.103 --> 00:07:10.468
Meg and Amy were beside themselves as they ran toward a tall thin man standing in the doorway.

78
00:07:10.469 --> 00:07:18.627
"Father! Father!" they screamed, as Mother stood still at the table, wiping her eyes with her napkin.

79
00:07:18.628 --> 00:07:25.145
"I'm home, my little girls," said Father happily as he hugged and kissed each of his daughters.

80
00:07:25.146 --> 00:07:32.427
Behind him stood Mr. Brookes, quiet as ever, but staring at only one person in the room: Meg.

81
00:07:32.428 --> 00:07:40.974
Lifting Beth from her chair and putting her on his lap, Father gathered his girls around him and said, "How I missed my little women."

82
00:07:40.975 --> 00:07:46.121
When Mr. Laurence, Laurie, and Mr. Brookes tried to leave unnoticed, Father called out,

83
00:07:46.122 --> 00:07:51.799
"Please stay. Tonight we're celebrating with both family and friends."

84
00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:59.737
And that is exactly what they did.

